Subject: [PATCH] session: export and import an authenticated client
 transport

Add ssh_session_export_transport(), ssh_session_import_transport(),
ssh_session_detach_transport() and ssh_session_get_channel(), so an
authenticated client session -- its keys, current IVs, sequence numbers,
negotiated algorithms, unconsumed input and open channels -- can move to
another process with its socket (passed separately, for instance by
SCM_RIGHTS) and continue the same connection. The server sees nothing.
OpenSSH's sshd moves the same packet state between its privilege-
separated processes.

The export reads the session and changes nothing, so a caller can resume
if the receiver fails; the detach then closes the exporter's copy of the
descriptor and marks the session dead, so ssh_disconnect() and
ssh_channel_free() send nothing. The export refuses compression (a zlib
stream has no serialisation) and answers SSH_AGAIN during a key exchange,
a partly read packet, unsent output or a pending request.

The import reuses the key exchange's own machinery: the negotiated names
go through crypt_set_algorithms_client(), the keys and the exporter's
current IVs through set_encrypt_key()/set_decrypt_key(), and the socket
is adopted as connected by the new ssh_socket_import_fd().
ssh_client_connection_callback() loses its static so the import can
install it for later rekeys.

Only the OpenSSL 3 backend is supported: EVP_CIPHER_CTX_get_updated_iv()
reports where AES-GCM's invocation counter and AES-CTR's block counter
stand. chacha20-poly1305 derives its nonce from the sequence number and
needs no IV. The libgcrypt and mbedTLS backends refuse the export.

Tested by moving a session into an exec'd process for chacha20-poly1305,
aes256-gcm, aes128-ctr with an ETM MAC and aes256-ctr with a plain MAC,
with data in flight in both libssh's channel buffer and the kernel, then
128 KiB each way across 10+ rekeys from both ends.

